  When in the course of hack events, it becomes necessary for one game to
dissolve the critical bonds which have connected it with others, and to
assume among the powers of the game, the separate and equal station to which
the levels of play and of player's skill entitle it, a decent respect to the
opinions of the net requires that it should declare the causes which impel
it to the separation.

  We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all hacks are not created
equal, that some are endowed by their creators with certain unalienable
traits, that among these are prayer, telepathy, and the polymorph of self.
That to utilize these traits, objects are distributed within dungeons,
deriving their magic powers from the minds of the developers, that whenever
any form of play becomes destructive to the game, it is the right of the
developers to alter or to abolish it, and to institute new magic, laying its
foundation on such principles and organizing its powers in such form, as to
the developers shall seem most likely to effect the integrity of the game.
Prudence, indeed, will dictate that traditions long established should not
be changed for light and transient causes; and accordingly all experience
hath shown, that hackers are more disposed to play while play is tolerable,
than to not play by abolishing the forms of the play to which they are
accustomed.  --Such play is the state of hack and the reason for this post.
Let a few facts be submitted to a candid bulletin board.
  Rogue, a computer fantasy game with a twist. It is crt oriented and the
object of the game is to survive the attacks of various monsters and get a
lot of gold, rather than the puzzle solving orientation of most computer
fantasy games. Indebted to M. Toy, K. Arnold, G. Wichman et al.

  Hack, originally very much rogue-like, later deformed to the game which is
now common. Twice the monsters and twice the fun. Indebted to A. Brouwer, J.
Fenlason, K. Woodland, M. Thome, J. Payne et al.

  NetHack, a continuation of the perversion of the hack sources. New and
powerful features contributed from the many strange people residing in the
UseNet Zone. Indebted to M. Stephenson, D. Kneller, G. Neiger, S. Turner and
K. Arromdee et al. (many others!)

  NewHack, the deformation continues. Developed as NetHack3.0 , with many
new features and extensions. Indebted to many (TBA). To be released real
soon now ...
